$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=3 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 3 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(3 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 3 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=3\\cdot\\frac{3}{3 x - 3} \\\\ &=\\frac{9}{3 x - 3} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv \\frac{12}{3 x - 3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=3 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 3 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{9}{3 x - 3}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}9 e^{- \\frac{y}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{3 x - 3} &\\overset{?}{=}9e^{-(3 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 3 \\right)})/(3)} \\\\ \\frac{9}{3 x - 3} &\\overset{?}{=}9e^{-\\ln(3 x - 3)} \\\\ \\frac{9}{3 x - 3} &=\\frac{3}{x - 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=3 \\ln{\\left(3 x - 3 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486 :topic/id #uuid "f8bc5d87-e505-5270-9efd-ad77aaf46e52" :topic/math-academy-id 1181 :topic/title "Verifying Solutions of Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93dc776f-23bf-56b1-9eee-5c4b2ad306a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/tex